---
// Lesson 2.2a — Spikes: Collisions, Health & Death
// Comparative analysis: How spikes are similar to and different from coins.
// OnCollisionEnter2D vs OnTriggerEnter2D, solid colliders, health tracking and death loop in StatManager.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.2a — Spikes: Collisions, Health &amp; Death</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    In lesson 2.1a you built coins that you pass through. Today you build spikes
    that you bump into. By asking two simple questions — <strong>how is this similar to the coin,
    and how is it different?</strong> — you unlock the second half of Unity's 2D physics engine:
    solid collisions, health tracking, and the death loop.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Comparative Thinking</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Computer scientists do not reinvent the wheel for every new feature. Whenever you encounter
        a new game mechanic, compare it to a problem you have already solved.
        Compare the Coin you built yesterday with the Spike you are building today:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Coin Pickup (Lesson 2.1a)</th><th>Hazardous Spike (Today)</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Physics Type</strong></td><td><strong>Trigger</strong> (<code>Is Trigger = true</code>). Intangible; player walks through.</td><td><strong>Solid Collision</strong> (<code>Is Trigger = false</code>). Physical wall; player bumps and stops.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Unity Message</strong></td><td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code></td><td><code>OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Parameter Received</strong></td><td><code>Collider2D other</code> (just the component touched).</td><td><code>Collision2D collision</code> (holds contact points, relative velocity, and collider).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Tag Check</strong></td><td><code>other.CompareTag("Coin")</code></td><td><code>collision.gameObject.CompareTag("Spikes")</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Stat Affected</strong></td><td>Increases wealth: <code>ChangeCoins(+1)</code></td><td>Decreases health: <code>ChangeHealth(-1)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Object Lifecycle</strong></td><td>Consumed on touch: <code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code></td><td>Persists forever: remains dangerous in the world.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Similarities &amp; Differences</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>How is the spike similar to the coin?</strong> Name at least two things both mechanics share in their logic flow.</li>
            <li><strong>How is the spike different from the coin?</strong> Why would a game feel completely broken if spikes had <code>Is Trigger = true</code> and destroyed themselves on touch like coins?</li>
            <li>In <code>OnCollisionEnter2D</code>, why do we write <code>collision.gameObject.CompareTag("Spikes")</code> instead of <code>collision.CompareTag(...)</code>? (Look closely at the parameter type: is <code>Collision2D</code> a GameObject, a Collider, or a data container?)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — Verify the Spike Collider.</strong> In <code>Assets/Prefabs/Spikes</code>:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Verify it has a <strong>BoxCollider2D</strong>.</li>
        <li>Ensure <strong>Is Trigger is unchecked</strong> (spikes are solid obstacles!).</li>
        <li>Ensure its Tag is set to <strong>Spikes</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — Add Health and Death to StatManager.cs.</strong>
    Open <code>Assets/Scripts/StatManager.cs</code> and expand it with health tracking:</p>
    <pre><code>    [SerializeField] private int health = 3;

    public void ChangeHealth(int amount)
    {
        health += amount;
        Debug.Log("Health Changed! New Health: " + health);

        if (health &lt;= 0)
        {
            Debug.Log("Player Died!");
            HandleDeath();
        }
    }

    private void HandleDeath()
    {
        // Halve coins rounded down as a death penalty
        coins = Mathf.FloorToInt(coins / 2f);
        health = 3;
        UnityEngine.SceneManagement.SceneManager.LoadScene("Start");
    }</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — Add OnCollisionEnter2D to PlayerController.cs.</strong>
    Open <code>PlayerController.cs</code> and add the collision handler right below <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>private void OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)
{
    if (collision.gameObject.CompareTag("Spikes"))
    {
        if (StatManager.manager != null)
        {
            StatManager.manager.ChangeHealth(-1);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Save your files and hit Play in Unity. Walk into a spike!
        Your player physically stops against the spike, and the Console logs <code>Health Changed! New Health: 2</code>.
        Hit it three times: health hits 0, your coins are halved, and the Start scene loads!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Collision vs. Trigger Bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If you accidentally check <strong>Is Trigger</strong> on the Spikes prefab, what happens when the player touches them? Does <code>OnCollisionEnter2D</code> ever fire?</li>
            <li>What happens if you delete <code>if (health &lt;= 0)</code> from <code>ChangeHealth</code>? What would health do if you kept bumping spikes indefinitely?</li>
            <li>Why does <code>HandleDeath()</code> use <code>Mathf.FloorToInt(coins / 2f)</code> instead of regular integer division? What would happen if a player had 1 coin when they died?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Exploring Collision Data</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In <code>OnCollisionEnter2D</code>, add <code>Debug.Log("Impact velocity: " + collision.relativeVelocity);</code>. Bump into a spike at different angles. What information does <code>Collision2D</code> give you that <code>Collider2D</code> could not?</li>
            <li>How could a game use <code>collision.relativeVelocity.magnitude</code> to create fall damage (only take damage if moving faster than 10 units/sec)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Articulate how spikes are similar to coins and how they are different</li>
            <li>Explain the difference between <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> and <code>OnCollisionEnter2D</code></li>
            <li>Explain why spikes use solid colliders (<code>Is Trigger = false</code>)</li>
            <li>Trace the execution of <code>ChangeHealth(-1)</code> through death and scene reload</li>
            <li>Commit your working spike hazard system to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — comprehensive guide to Unity 2D collision events</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/index.html">Unit 2: Stats &amp; Pickups</a> — the StatManager in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — physics callbacks</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="2.3a.html">2.3a — Health Potions &amp; the Max-Health Cap</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="2.2a" title="2.2a Spikes: Collisions, Health &amp; Death — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
